To fix the problem you're experiencing while testing async code with Jest, you need to ensure that your test properly handles the promises returned by the asynchronous code. In your posted code, it appears that you are trying to test an asynchronous operation that is expected to reject. Here are the steps you should follow to resolve the issue:

1. **Return the Promise**: Make sure to return the promise from the test. This is crucial because if you do not return it, the test will finish before the promise resolves or rejects, potentially leading to misleading results. You can achieve this by modifying your test to return the promise chain. For your case, it would look something like this:

   ```javascript
   return request(app)
       .post("/sessions")
       .send({ email: "gustavo@gmail.com", password: "gustavo" })
       .then(responseToken => {
           const { token } = responseToken.body;

           return request(app)
               .post("/users")
               .set({ Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` })
               .send({
                   email: "testIntegration@test.com.br",
                   name: "Test ",
                   lastName: "Integration",
                   password: "test",
           });
       })
       .then(userResponse => {
           expect(userResponse).rejects.toEqual(
               new AppError("User is not an Admin!")
           );
       });
   ```

   Make sure to implement the returning of the promise as shown above; otherwise, Jest may finalize the test before the async operation completes, which defeats the purpose of your test and could result in an unhandled promise rejection or other unexpected errors. This recommendation comes from the standard practice that states you should return the promise when dealing with asynchronous tests in Jest [SO-44693655].

2. **Using `async/await`**: Alternatively, if you want to use `async/await`, you can structure your test like this:

   ```javascript
   test('description of your test', async () => {
       const responseToken = await request(app)
           .post("/sessions")
           .send({ email: "gustavo@gmail.com", password: "gustavo" });

       const { token } = responseToken.body;

       const userResponse = await request(app)
           .post("/users")
           .set({ Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` })
           .send({
               email: "testIntegration@test.com.br",
               name: "Test ",
               lastName: "Integration",
               password: "test",
           });

       expect(userResponse).rejects.toEqual(
           new AppError("User is not an Admin!")
       );
   });
   ```

   Note that for this approach to work, the `expect` statement should be modified since currently, you're expecting a resolved promise when `rejects` expects a rejected promise. This distinction is crucial in ensuring proper promise handling.

Make sure you also handle any potential exceptions surrounding your asynchronous operations, as this can prevent unhandled promise rejections [SO-47056421]. 

These changes should help you fix the issues you are facing with your async test in Jest.

